        "### **7.1 Classification Findings**\n",
        "Both decision trees rely on the same small set of attributes, and these are almost entirely academic and financial. In the Gini tree, the number of courses approved in the first semester is the first split and accounts for 61% of the total importance; in the Entropy tree it accounts for 50.7%. The top five attributes are the same in both trees: first-semester approved courses, tuition fees up to date, second-semester grade, second-semester evaluations, and first-semester enrolled courses. No attribute outside academic performance and tuition payment appears in the top five.\n",
